D. Path Optimization
The output from our Timed A* algorithm is a path on Tτ∗ consisting of a series of triangles that form a channel. We find the shortest path in the channel that ensures sufficient clearance. Funnel algorithm [37], [38], [39] can be used for computing a shortest path within a simple triangulated polygon connecting the start and goal points. To account for non-zero radius obstacles, [26] proposed a modified funnel algorithm by inserting conceptual circles at each vertex of the channel. The resulting path is composed of alternating straight-line segments and arcs. In our work, we further extend this method to account for the pedestrian dynamics. Given a pedestrian piτ , we project the velocity viτ onto the vector eji = pj − pi, where pj is the other end of the edge
